## Lore

In the context of this game we concern ourselves only with the most
sophisticated, powerful and human-like machines: machines that either supplement
the fragile human body or act completely on their own, as mechanized facsimiles
of the living, driven by an artificial intelligence.

As a robot character the player builds and designs the automaton, limited only
by budget and by the technology available. You can play an ordinary human who
dons a robot suit, pilot a giant robot or robot vehicle, or be a synthetic
entity - a machine with an artificial intelligence. The appearance can be almost
anything: humanoid, animal, insect-like, or vehicular. The weapons and the
powers - flight, strength and the rest - are decided by spending the budget.

**Type 1, Mechanical Body** is humanoid or vehicular and requires a pilot. It is
the least expensive type because it needs no costly artificial intelligence, and
it must be large enough to hold its operator - at least the size of a mid-size
automobile. The pilot works it through levers, joysticks, pedals, buttons and
even a steering wheel, which sounds primitive and is no harder than flying a jet;
with practice it moves as fluidly as any other type, and voice actuation or a
telemental helmet improves that further. A character trained on one has a 60%
chance of being able to pilot any Type 1 - but in an unfamiliar machine the
controls, equipment and response differ, so attacks per melee are capped at two
and initiative is never gained.

**Type 2, Robot** is a machine with or without a human pilot, and requires an
artificial intelligence. **Type 3, Exoskeleton** is a robot suit that enhances
its wearer, and is an extension of the pilot: his physical and combat abilities
are the exoskeleton''s. **Type 4, Android** looks and acts human, and requires an
artificial intelligence.

## GM Notes

**The robot budget**, printed 136 - all of it must be spent:

| roll | budget | roll | budget |
|---|---|---|---|
| 01-15 | $7.5 million | 60-74 | $10 million |
| 16-30 | $8.5 million | 75-89 | $11 million |
| 31-44 | $9 million | 90-00 | $12 million |
| 45-59 | $9.5 million | | |

The pilot is created like any other character - attributes, hit points,
alignment - but does **not** roll for psionics or super powers. Hand to hand
combat is not automatic and must be selected as a learned skill; every hero gets
two attacks per melee and more come from hand to hand.

Other than the robot itself the character has only conventional weapons, armour
and equipment. High-tech items are accessible only to someone still affiliated
with the sponsoring organization.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'hu-robotics');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'hu-robotics';

